## GEOMORPOLOGY and SURFICAL GEOLOY

Based on topography, surface roughness, and orientation of roughness elements four main regional physiographic elements are identified on the digital elevation model by: (1) the Niagara Escarpment and cuesta on the west, (2) drumlinized Newmarket Till, (3) steep-walled tunnel channels, (3) ORM, and (4) smooth Halton Till plain (Fig. 6). Within the 900 km\({}^{2}\) Humber River watershed, the surficial sediment consists of Oak Ridges Moraine (140 km\({}^{2}\), 15%), Halton Till (450 km\({}^{2}\), 50%), and bedrock, where Newmarket Till and glaciolactus-trine units account for the remaining 250 km\({}^{2}\) (35%). In the watershed, the ORM forms an east-west ridge that has a maximum elevation of _ca._ 420 m asl where it onlaps the Niagara Escarpment, and a relief of _ca._ 100 m above the Halton Till plain to the south. The moraine ranges in width from 3 to 24 km (Fig. 7) and has an asymmetric cross-section with a steeper, _ca._ 3\({}^{\circ}\) northern flank that is commonly <5 km long, and a gentler southern flank that extends for 10-20 km. The steepest ORM slopes are _ca._ 14\({}^{\circ}\), but about 97% of the area has slopes <5\({}^{\circ}\). Steep slopes commonly occur where Halton Till crops out along the sides of stream valleys. Adjacent to the Niagara Escarpment, the ORM surface sediment is sand and the landscape consists of disorganized, internally drained depressions and fluvially dissected topography (Fig. 7A). To the east, the ORM is extensively incised where sand crops out (Fig. 7B), but is significantly less dissected where Halton Till is exposed (Fig. 7D-E).

